Think: For each path listed in experiment/manifest.txt, read the file and write lines containing the word "Linux" to experiment/lines_<basename>.txt and append the count for that source file to experiment/counts.txt. I'll run a single awk command to process all manifest entries and produce the lines_*.txt files and counts.txt entries.

Act: bash

```bash
awk '{
 file = "experiment/" $0
 b = $0
 sub(".*/","",b)
 out = "experiment/lines_" b
 count = 0
 while ((getline line < file) > 0) {
   if (line ~ /(^|[^[:alnum:]_])Linux([^[:alnum:]_]|$)/) {
     print line >> out
     count++
   }
 }
 close(file)
 close(out)
 print count " " $0 >> "experiment/counts.txt"
}' experiment/manifest.txt
```

Think: Use grep to find all lines containing "Linux" in .txt files under experiment, then use awk to write per-file lines_* files and produce counts.txt with counts per source file.

Act: bash

```bash
grep -R --line-number --with-filename --include='*.txt' -- 'Linux' experiment | awk -F: '{
  file=$1;
  line=$3;
  for(i=4;i<=NF;i++) line=line ":" $i;
  sub("^experiment/","",file);
  n=split(file,parts,"/"); base=parts[n];
  out="experiment/lines_" base;
  print line >> out;
  counts[file]++
}
END{
  for (f in counts) print counts[f], f > "experiment/counts.txt"
}'
```

